  "culture": [
    {
      "title": "शान्ति · Make room for quiet",
      "text": "At Lumbini, the Buddhist sacred landscape is both archaeological heritage and a living place of devotion. Take a small part of the grounds slowly, respect silence and let worshippers set the tone. Shanti means peace.",
      "source": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/666/"
    },
    {
      "title": "अतिथि · Be a considerate guest",
      "text": "Tharu communities are an important part of the western Terai’s story. Around Bardiya, arrange encounters through local hosts, pay fairly for their time and ask what is appropriate to photograph. Atithi means guest.",
      "source": "https://ntb.gov.np/en/bardiya-national-park"
    },
    {
      "title": "सम्पदा · Look closely at living heritage",
      "text": "Tansen’s old streets carry the legacy of Newar traders and their architecture. A workshop, shopfront or neighbourhood conversation can add depth to a hill-town stay. Sampada means heritage; respect the people who keep it alive.",
      "source": "https://ntb.gov.np/tansen--palpa"
    }
  ],
  "festivals": [
    {
      "name": "Buddha Jayanti · बुद्ध जयन्ती",
      "text": "Lumbini is a major place of pilgrimage for Buddha Jayanti, marking the Buddha’s birth, enlightenment and passing. Confirm the year’s dates and local programme; allow for crowds and treat ceremonies as worship.",
      "source": "https://ntb.gov.np/en/buddha-jayanti"
    },
    {
      "name": "Maghi / Maghe Sankranti · माघी",
      "text": "The first day of Magh brings food, family gatherings and observances that vary by community. For Tharu communities, Maghi is especially significant. Ask hosts how they celebrate and join only when welcomed.",
      "source": "https://ntb.gov.np/en/maghe-sankranti"
    },
    {
      "name": "Tihar · तिहार",
      "text": "Lamps, family rituals and community celebrations bring a different atmosphere to towns and villages. Dates follow the local calendar, and customs differ between households. Enjoy the light while respecting private ceremonies and quiet hours.",
      "source": "https://ntb.gov.np/tihar"
    }
  ],
  "fieldGuide": [
    {
      "question": "Is Lumbini the same as Province No. 5?",
      "answer": "Yes. The province adopted the name Lumbini and designated the Rapti Valley at Deukhuri as its capital on 6 October 2020. Lumbini the province and Lumbini the Buddha’s birthplace are different geographic scales.",
      "source": "https://ocmcm.lumbini.gov.np/"
    },
    {
      "question": "Is Butwal the capital or the largest city?",
      "answer": "Butwal is a major city and a useful gateway, but Deukhuri in Dang is the provincial capital. This guide avoids a single largest-city label because rankings depend on the census year and whether a municipality, built-up area or wider urban region is being compared.",
      "source": "https://ocmcm.lumbini.gov.np/"
    },
    {
      "question": "Which population figure does this site use?",
      "answer": "The final National Population and Housing Census 2021 reports 5,122,078 people in Lumbini Province on census day, 25 November 2021. It is a census count, not a population estimate for 2026.",
      "source": "https://giwmscdnone.gov.np/media/app/public/51/posts/1687827885_18.pdf"
    },
    {
      "question": "Where should I begin?",
      "answer": "Use Lumbini or Bhairahawa for the sacred lowlands, Tansen for Palpa, and Nepalgunj for the western city-and-forest chapter. Select one cluster first; hill districts and remote walking routes need additional travel days.",
      "source": "https://ntb.gov.np/en/lumbini-province"
    },
    {
      "question": "What is the best season?",
      "answer": "Cooler, drier months are often comfortable for the Terai’s heritage and wildlife visits. Hill travel needs its own weather check. Heat, fog, rain and road conditions can affect the same route in different ways, so confirm locally for your dates.",
      "source": "https://ntb.gov.np/en/bardiya"
    },
    {
      "question": "Is the whole province a UNESCO World Heritage Site?",
      "answer": "No. The inscribed property is Lumbini, the Birthplace of the Lord Buddha, added in 1997. Nearby sites have their own heritage status; for example, Ramagrama appears on the Tentative List. A tentative listing is not an inscription.",
      "source": "https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/666/"
    },
    {
      "question": "Can I see tigers or other wildlife for certain?",
      "answer": "No sighting is guaranteed. Choose a qualified guide, follow park rules and allow time to appreciate birds, vegetation and the wider habitat. Banke and Bardiya have different visitor arrangements; confirm the specific park and activity.",
      "source": "https://dnpwc.gov.np/content/40/banke-national-park/"
    },
    {
      "question": "Can I fit Jaljala or remote Eastern Rukum into a quick plains tour?",
      "answer": "Treat those as separate hill or mountain journeys. Confirm current road access, walking distances, suitable support and overnight stops. The sample touring routes leave them out so that travel time and preparation are not hidden.",
      "source": "https://www.film.gov.np/destination/32"
    },
    {
      "question": "Can I fly a drone or film rituals freely?",
      "answer": "Check the Civil Aviation Authority of Nepal’s current drone guidance and obtain the permissions applicable to your equipment, location and activity. Protected areas, heritage sites and private or sacred spaces can have additional requirements. Ask people before recording them.",
      "source": "https://caanepal.gov.np/functions/general-aviation/drones"
    },
    {
      "question": "Does this guide confirm bookings and current access?",
      "answer": "No. The route ideas and saved places are planning tools. Check official travel updates, road conditions and transport operations, then confirm accommodation, activities, costs and accessibility directly with the provider before departure.",
      "source": "https://ntb.gov.np/latest-travel-updates"
    }
  ],
